<binaryimage arch="x86:LE:64:default:gcc">
<!--
    kuna re-bytecode-append-call-loses: a call renders with fewer arguments
    than the disassembly sets up, and the missing registers are the ones
    written BEFORE the branch the call sits behind.

    Funcdata::onlyOpUse (funcdata_varnode.cc:1851) walks every descendant of
    the Varnode reaching a CALL in a candidate argument register and rejects
    the trial on any CPUI_LOAD or CPUI_STORE.  A container append compiled with
    the one-element fast path inlined and the grow path left as a call sets
    both of the call's arguments up before the capacity test, because both arms
    need them; the fast path then dereferences exactly those Varnodes, and the
    trials sink.  No execution path runs both arms, so neither access can
    compete with the call.

    The vector is sub_140004540 of vm.exe (challenge
    69b7492049fa49a2a260218f, stripped MSVC x86-64 PE) whose
    sub_140007be0(container, end, src) call at 0x1400046cc renders
    sub_140007be0(a0), reduced to one branch, re-spelled in the SysV ABI and
    assembled from
    decompiler/crates/kuna-analysis/tests/fixtures/exclusivearguse_x86_64.s:

        401024  lea  0xfd5(%rip),%rdx     ; the source byte,  3rd argument
        40102e  mov  0x8(%rdi),%rsi       ; the write cursor, 2nd argument
        401032  cmp  0x10(%rdi),%rsi
        401036  je   401044
        401038  movzbl (%rdx),%eax        ; fast path: LOAD through rdx
        40103b  mov  %al,(%rsi)           ;            STORE through rsi
        401042  jmp  401049
        401044  call append_slow          ; the other arm

    append_loop is the control: the same two arms inside a loop, where the back
    edge makes each arm reachable from the other, so the rule declines and the
    call keeps its one argument in both passes.  append_slow leaves rcx
    untouched, which is what keeps the calleearity family from reconstructing
    the list from the callee body instead.

    Phase: P4 active input-trial scoring, option exclusivearguse off|on.  Pass 1
    (off) pins the bug: append_slow() with no arguments.  Pass 2 (on, the
    shipped default) pins all three.  Nothing else moves.
-->
